\section{Derivations}
\label{sec:derivations}

In this section we derive the core equations of informational cosmology from
basic assumptions about the Primordial Informational Field (PIF) and its
relation to emergent geometry and black hole growth.

\subsection{Derivation of the Informational Field Equation (IFE)}

We begin by postulating that the PIF is characterized by an informational
density $\rho_I(t,\mathbf{x})$ and flow velocity $\mathbf{v}_I(t,\mathbf{x})$.
Conservation of informational content in a comoving volume $V$ implies
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dt} \int_V \rho_I\, d^3x
= - \int_{\partial V} \rho_I \mathbf{v}_I \cdot d\mathbf{S}
+ \int_V S_I\, d^3x,
\label{eq:conservation}
\end{equation}
where $S_I$ denotes a source term accounting for non-linear informational
production or annihilation. Applying the divergence theorem to the flux term
and requiring Eq.~(\ref{eq:conservation}) to hold for arbitrary $V$ yields
\begin{equation}
\partial_t \rho_I + \nabla \cdot (\rho_I \mathbf{v}_I) = S_I.
\label{eq:continuity_general}
\end{equation}
We model $S_I$ as a combination of divergence-driven and quadratic
self-interaction contributions,
\begin{equation}
S_I = \alpha (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{v}_I) + \beta \rho_I^2,
\label{eq:source}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are transport coefficients determined by the
microscopic properties of the PIF. Substituting Eq.~(\ref{eq:source}) into
Eq.~(\ref{eq:continuity_general}) yields the Informational Field Equation (IFE),
\begin{equation}
\partial_t \rho_I + \nabla \cdot (\rho_I \mathbf{v}_I)
= \alpha (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{v}_I) + \beta \rho_I^2.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Emergent Metric from Informational Gradients}

To derive the Emergent Metric Equation (EME), we assume that spacetime geometry
is a coarse-grained manifestation of informational gradients. At leading order,
the metric tensor $g_{\mu\nu}$ is expanded around a background metric
$g^{(0)}_{\mu\nu}$ as
\begin{equation}
g_{\mu\nu} = g^{(0)}_{\mu\nu}
+ A\,\partial_\mu \partial_\nu \rho_I
+ B\,(\partial_\mu \rho_I)(\partial_\nu \rho_I),
\label{eq:metric_expansion}
\end{equation}
where $A$ and $B$ are phenomenological coefficients. This form is motivated by
the requirement that geometry respond to second-order variations in $\rho_I$
and that regions of strong informational gradients correspond to high
curvature.

Curvature invariants follow from Eq.~(\ref{eq:metric_expansion}) via standard
differential geometric constructions. At leading order in gradients of
$\rho_I$, the Ricci scalar $R$ and Weyl invariant $C^2$ take the schematic
forms
\begin{equation}
R = c_1 \Box \rho_I + c_2 (\partial \rho_I)^2,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
C^2 = d_1 (\partial \partial \rho_I)^2 + d_2 (\partial \rho_I)^4,
\end{equation}
where $(c_1, c_2, d_1, d_2)$ are determined by $(A,B)$ and the background
geometry. These relations establish the curvature--information duality:
high-curvature regions correspond to rapidly varying informational density,
rather than singularities in the metric.

\subsection{Derivation of the SMBH Informational Sink Equation (SISE)}

We model black holes as sinks of informational density. Let $M_{\rm BH}(t)$
denote the black hole mass and $\rho_I$ the local informational density near
its effective horizon. The rate of mass increase is assumed proportional to
the informational flux through an effective capture area $A_{\rm eff}$,
\begin{equation}
\dot{M}_{\rm BH} \propto \rho_I A_{\rm eff}.
\label{eq:bh_flux}
\end{equation}
Introducing a conversion coefficient $\gamma$ and writing
$A_{\rm eff} = A_0 (1+\delta)$, where $A_0$ is a baseline area and $\delta$
encodes chaotic inflow amplification, we obtain
\begin{equation}
\dot{M}_{\rm BH} = \gamma\,\rho_I\,A_0(1+\delta).
\end{equation}
Integrating from an initial time $t_0$ with seed mass $M_{\rm seed}$ yields
\begin{equation}
M_{\rm BH}(t) = M_{\rm seed} 
+ \gamma A_0 (1+\delta) \int_{t_0}^{t} \rho_I(t')\,dt',
\end{equation}
demonstrating that SMBH mass is the time-integral of local informational
density. In regimes where $\rho_I$ and $(1+\delta)$ are approximately constant,
this reduces to a linear growth law,
\begin{equation}
M_{\rm BH}(t) \approx M_{\rm seed} + K (t - t_0),
\end{equation}
with $K = \gamma \rho_I A_0 (1+\delta)$, consistent with the super-Eddington
growth rates observed in simulation.